{% extends '_layout.jinja' %}
{% import 'components.jinja' as ui with context %}
{% import '_code.jinja' as code with context %}
{% block title %}{{ homepage.title }}{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
{# Station IDs retain links from the original scope diagram. #}
{% set stations = [
  {'id': 'input', 'name': 'Input', 'line': 'Bytes off the socket',
   'detail': 'A Tokio TCP stream. The server reads first, under a 10-second deadline by default: a preamble if it expects one, otherwise the first byte.'},
  {'id': 'framing', 'name': 'Framing', 'line': 'Cut at the length prefix',
   'detail': 'The default frame codec reads a 4-byte big-endian length prefix and rejects payloads over 1,024 bytes.'},
  {'id': 'envelope', 'name': 'Envelope', 'line': 'Read the envelope',
   'detail': 'The serializer decodes an Envelope: a message ID, an optional correlation ID, and the payload bytes. Trailing bytes are a decoding error.'},
  {'id': 'reassembly', 'name': 'Reassembly', 'line': 'Fragments, rejoined', 'optional': true,
   'detail': 'With fragmentation enabled, fragments arrive inside envelopes and are rejoined before routing, bounded by size, by count, and by time.'},
  {'id': 'deflection', 'name': 'Route', 'line': 'Route by message ID',
   'detail': 'One handler per ID. An ID with no route is logged and dropped; registering an ID twice fails at setup.'},
  {'id': 'modulation', 'name': 'Middleware', 'line': 'Wrap the handler',
   'detail': 'The route’s middleware wraps its handler, so it sees the request on the way in and the reply on the way out.'},
  {'id': 'dwell', 'name': 'Handler', 'line': 'Handle the message',
   'detail': 'The handler runs with the envelope it was sent. It returns nothing; the default reply echoes the payload.'},
  {'id': 'retrace', 'name': 'Reply', 'line': 'Serialize, frame, send',
   'detail': 'The reply keeps the request’s correlation ID and leaves through optional fragmentation, the serializer, the codec’s wrapper, the before_send hook, and the encoder.'},
] %}
{% set capabilities = [
  {'name': 'Message routes', 'line': 'By message ID, one handler each', 'href': '/wireframe/guides/routing/'},
  {'name': 'Frame codecs', 'line': 'Define the boundaries of a frame', 'href': '/wireframe/guides/codecs/'},
  {'name': 'Middleware', 'line': 'Wraps every route, in and out', 'href': '/wireframe/guides/middleware/'},
  {'name': 'Client', 'line': 'The same codec and envelope, outbound', 'href': '/wireframe/guides/client/'},
] %}

<section class="wf-shell wf-opening" aria-labelledby="hero-title"><div><p class="wf-marque">Common Signal</p><h1 id="hero-title" class="wf-hero__title"><img src="/wireframe/assets/logos/wordmark-1400.webp" srcset="/wireframe/assets/logos/wordmark-700.webp 700w, /wireframe/assets/logos/wordmark-1400.webp 1400w" sizes="(width < 640px) 90vw, 38rem" alt="wireframe" width="1400" height="357" fetchpriority="high"></h1>
<p class="wf-principle">What we connect, we should understand.</p>
<p>Wireframe is an experimental Rust library for building servers and clients for frame-based binary protocols. Frame the bytes, route messages by ID, and inspect the work at each boundary.</p>
<p class="wf-status"><a href="{{ docs_url }}">Published API · {{ published_api_version }}</a> · These guides are in development alongside <a href="{{ alpha_pr }}">{{ wireframe_version }}</a>.</p>
<div class="wf-actions">{{ ui.button('Get started', '/wireframe/getting-started/') }}{{ ui.button('Read the architecture', '/wireframe/architecture/', kind='quiet') }}</div></div>
{{ ui.plate('29', eager=true, playable=true) }}</section>
<section class="wf-chapter wf-shell" aria-labelledby="capabilities-title"><p class="wf-label">I · The visible city</p><h2 id="capabilities-title">A common foundation for unfamiliar traffic.</h2>
<p>Every protocol brings its own rules. Wireframe provides framing and routing machinery so applications can concentrate on the messages they handle.</p>
<ul class="wf-links">{% for c in capabilities %}<li><a href="{{ c.href }}">{{ c.name }}</a><p>{{ c.line }}</p></li>{% endfor %}</ul>
</section>
<section class="wf-chapter wf-shell" aria-labelledby="journey-title"><p class="wf-label">II · The public machine</p><h2 id="journey-title">Follow the message.</h2>
<p>A codec identifies frame boundaries. The router selects one handler by message ID. Middleware surrounds the handler and can change its reply.</p>
<nav class="wf-path" aria-label="Simplified request and reply path">
  <ol>{% for s in stations %}<li><a href="#station-{{ s.id }}">{{ s.name }}{% if s.optional %} (optional){% endif %}</a></li>{% endfor %}</ol>
</nav>
<ol class="wf-stages">{% for s in stations %}<li id="station-{{ s.id }}"><strong>{{ s.line }}</strong><p>{{ s.detail }}</p></li>{% endfor %}</ol>
<p>These stages simplify the sample server at {{ sample_revision[:8] }}. The <a href="/wireframe/architecture/">architecture guide</a> also covers optional message assembly.</p>
<h3 id="steps-title">Describe the messages and register a route</h3><div class="wf-pair">
{{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'messages', title='first_server.rs · messages') }}
{{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'app', title='first_server.rs · routes') }}</div>
<p>Both panels come from <a href="/wireframe/getting-started/">the complete example</a>, checked against {{ samples_verified_against }} on {{ samples_verified_on }}.</p>
<h3>Keep the protocol’s identity.</h3><p>The proposed native adapter carries protocol-specific headers, correlation and reply context through the runtime. Envelope conversion becomes an optional bridge.</p>
<a href="{{ development_base }}/docs/rfcs/rfc-0003-native-message-adapter-boundary.md">Proposed · RFC 0003 · Read the proposal</a>
<h3>Give each direction room to move.</h3><p>The proposed session driver coordinates reads, writes, application work and cancellation under pressure.</p>
<a href="{{ development_base }}/docs/rfcs/rfc-0004-duplex-session-driver.md">Proposed · RFC 0004 · Review the proposal</a>
</section>
<section class="wf-chapter wf-shell" aria-labelledby="world-title"><p class="wf-label">III · Bounds and budgets</p><h2 id="world-title">Capacity is part of the design.</h2>
<div class="wf-pair"><div><p>A message leaving a queue can still occupy memory. The proposed budget model accounts for retained data and admitted work until the accountable ownership ends.</p>
<a href="{{ development_base }}/docs/rfcs/rfc-0007-budgets-scheduling-and-assembly.md">Proposed · RFC 0007 · Review the budget model</a>
<p>The <a href="/wireframe/guides/large-messages/">large-message guide</a> describes the sample revision’s fragment limits and assembly byte caps.</p></div>{{ ui.plate('15', playable=true) }}</div>
</section>
<section class="wf-chapter wf-shell" aria-labelledby="mxd-title"><p class="wf-label">IV · Operations in the open</p><h2 id="mxd-title">Show the working.</h2>
<div class="wf-pair"><div><p>Run the <a href="/wireframe/examples/">examples</a>, inspect the <a href="{{ development_base }}/tests">test fixtures</a> and use the <a href="/wireframe/guides/testing/">testing guide</a> to check what happens when a frame arrives one byte at a time.</p><a href="/wireframe/scope/">Explore the Common Signal field guide</a></div>{{ ui.plate('25', playable=true) }}</div>
</section>
<section class="wf-chapter wf-shell" aria-labelledby="install-title"><p class="wf-label">Next steps</p><h2 id="install-title">Build systems that can be understood.</h2>
<div class="wf-actions">{{ ui.button('Get started', '/wireframe/getting-started/') }}{{ ui.button('Follow the roadmap', '/wireframe/roadmap/', kind='quiet') }}</div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
